"**Limits to plan for:** a textual-clean merge can break the build; unlike bors-ng's exact-merge testing, this policy gives up merge-result validation to avoid a new CI run and review. Main's push CI tests the result only after someone pushes; the helper never pushes. In nana-pi, the next land refuses until captured main has a green push run. Required macOS and Windows suite and install-check legs block; a Linux-only failure can leave CI green. Lands sharing one review-ledger home serialize under its lock and recheck captured main, branch, tracked state and source tip inside it. Different ledger homes and external operations do not share that lock; the final-check-to-ff instant remains undefended. An item in flight on a Windows seat whose key came from a short or wrongly cased main checkout restarts its round count once (W7c closed the key itself; a round already recorded under the old key still needs a re-review under the new one). A firmlink-typed `--out` on macOS (`/System/Volumes/Data/...`) stays as today. Deploy with no land, review, or worker process running. While a land holds the ledger lock, another review that waits past the 15-second limit is refused at admission or its completed verdict goes unrecorded; keep its output and re-run it. A new lane-authored commit—including an amend, rebase result, or empty commit—starts with a clean verdict record and needs its own review. A narrow review counts as a LAND for the whole ancestry up to its revision. A direct commit to `main` bypasses this helper; the working pattern forbids direct commits to `main`. A review's verdict comes from the final non-blank line when its canonical form contains a bounded `verdict:` label; otherwise it may come from the first non-blank line, but only when that raw line is plain VERDICT: WORD with an optional whole-number score such as (9/10), — 9/10 or 9/10, and an optional final period. Before detection, the parser removes one leading byte-order mark, removes Unicode default-ignorables, folds selected visual look-alikes, applies NFKC, repeats the removal and fold, then lower-cases. A selected final line may use the familiar leading marks and trailing punctuation; its raw text must still match the final-line grammar. A plain first-line verdict is refused if any other non-blank line mentions “verdict”; when the final line is selected, only the first line is checked for a conflicting verdict; a verdict in the middle is not read, so `notes`, `VERDICT: SEND-BACK`, `VERDICT: LAND` records LAND. A first-line verdict-like label must be valid and carry the same word. Thus VERDICT: LAND 9/10 followed by notes records LAND, but a quoted or marked first line, a later correction, a disguised conflicting label, or a malformed selected line records an empty verdict. A first-line verdict followed by a correction that avoids the word “verdict” can still be read as LAND; middle-only verdicts, misspelled labels, and look-alikes outside the configured table are not detected. Unicode default-ignorable handling follows the running Node version's Unicode tables. An empty verdict stays on the revision and refuses it for good; both launchers print one escaped diagnostic line and exit 1 after consuming the round, so the fix is a new commit and a fresh review, not a re-run. The diagnostic line has no length cap.": 0 matches
'**Verifier-pipe guard (user-scope):** every session will prompt before running a Claude Code Bash or PowerShell command when a masked pipeline\'s exit status can reach the commit without earlier active `pipefail`. Earlier live Bash text also makes that pipeline prompt when it contains `errexit` anywhere, in any case and even inside a longer word, or contains a `set` word at any position followed within the same simple command—before the next `;`, `&`, `|`, newline or closing bracket—by an option word made of `-` and letters including `e`. A later `set +e` or `set +o errexit` never turns that text flag off, and `+o errexit` itself counts as enabling, even before a status-discarding `;` or newline. Without that earlier Bash errexit text, a `;` or a newline that discards the status does not prompt, and `git.exe` and path spellings count as git; on native Windows the guard covers PowerShell too, through the pipe guard\'s own PowerShell dialect (string, here-string and comment rules). This is an additive best-effort text check: a pipeline or commit not present in the literal command text and reached through a later call is not matched; the sandbox is the boundary. Claude Code documents the response shape as `hookSpecificOutput` with `hookEventName: "PreToolUse"`, `permissionDecision: "ask"`, and `permissionDecisionReason` ([PreToolUse hook output](https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/claude-code/hooks#pretooluse-decision-control)). Non-matches and hook errors abstain (empty stdout), so Claude Code\'s normal permission flow continues; errors write a diagnostic to stderr.': 0 matches
