"Lane mode holds an exclusive-create pid lock file for the worker lifetime. Any existing lock refuses the launch, naming its path and recorded pid when readable; a later launch never reclaims it automatically. The hard-stop watcher releases only a lock that still names its pi-worker and only after the recorded builder group is gone; on Windows it keeps the lock for a builder launched through `cmd.exe`. A builder's tool command in its own process group can still outlive that builder group. Before removing a lock by hand, confirm that no builder process group or tool process for that worktree is running. The lane wall-clock ceiling is **28,800 seconds (8 hours)**; `--max-secs N` overrides it for one launch. The ceiling kills the entire builder process group and reports FAILED. If no affirmative trust record covers the worktree, the launcher prints `trust: none for <worktree> — project post-edit checks are inert (nana-setup trust <dir>)` and continues; project post-edit commands are inert until pi trusts the directory. After taking the lock, a lane build appends a start record before the builder starts and an end record before releasing the lock on each handled exit. Its final build-record stderr line gives this build's outcome, elapsed minutes and recorded cost, followed by the lane summary.": 0 matches
